1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,803
DDR4 and DDR5 sticks both have 288 pins and the same length.

2
00:00:07,083 --> 00:00:09,164
So why won't one fit the other's slot?

3
00:00:09,914 --> 00:00:11,789
Look at the gap in the bottom edge.

4
00:00:12,069 --> 00:00:15,152
That's the notch, and the slot has a matching ridge.

5
00:00:15,432 --> 00:00:18,399
On DDR5, it sits in a different spot.

6
00:00:19,149 --> 00:00:20,646
It's a key, on purpose.

7
00:00:20,926 --> 00:00:27,485
The generations use different voltages and signals, so the wrong stick is blocked before it can do harm.

8
00:00:28,235 --> 00:00:33,372
To check your board, look up its model and read the memory line on its spec page.

9
00:00:33,652 --> 00:00:36,406
CPU-Z shows the type you already have.

10
00:00:37,156 --> 00:00:44,240
Shortcut: AMD's AM5 boards take only DDR5, AM4 only DDR4.

11
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:48,591
Intel's twelfth to fourteenth gen boards come in both, so check.

12
00:00:49,341 --> 00:00:54,152
Every generation is in our full video, Every Type of RAM Explained.

